\pard\ul\b 1\ulnone . Introduction\par

\pard\li284\b0 1.1. Purpose\par

\pard\li568 The purpose of this specification is to define a lossless compressed data format that :\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-152\li994 Is independent of CPU type, operating system, file system, and character set, and hence can be used for interchange;\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Can be produced or consumed, even for an arbitrarily long sequentially presented input data stream, using only an a priori bounded amount of intermediate storage, and hence can be used in data communications or similar structures such as Unix filters;\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Can use a number of different compression methods;\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Can be implemented readily in a manner not covered by patents, and hence can be practiced freely.\par

\pard\li568 The data format defined by this specification does not attempt to allow random access to compressed data.\par
\par

\pard\li284\lang1041 1.2. Intended audience\par

\pard\li568 This specification is indended for use by implementors of software to compress data into zlib  format and/or decompress data from zlib format.\par
The text of the specification assumes a basic background in progtamming at tle level of bits and other primitive data representations.\par

\pard\li284\par
1.3. Scope\par

\pard\li568 The specification specifies a compressed data format that can be used for in-memory compression of a sequence of arbitrary bytes.\par
\par

\pard\li284 1.4. Compliance\par

\pard\li568 Unless otherwise indicated below, a compliant decompressor must be able to accept and decompress any data set that conforms to all the specifications presented here; a compliant compressor must produce data sets that conforn to all the specifications presented here.\par
\par

\pard\li284 1.5. Definitions of terms and conventions used\par

\pard\li568 byte : 8 bits stored or transmissed as a unit (same as an octet). (For this specification, a byte is exactly 8 bits, even on machines which store a character on a number of bits different from 8.) See below, for the numbering within a byte.\par
\par

\pard\li284 1.6. Changes from previous versions\par

\pard\li568 Version 3.1 was the first public release of this specification. In version 3.2, some terminology was changed and the Adler-32 sample code was rewritten for clarity. In version 3.3, the support for a preset dictionary was introduced, and the specification was converted to RFC style.\par
\par

