\pard\li284 2.1. Overall conventions\par

\pard\li568 In the diagram below, a box like this :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1032 
\pard\intbl\li852\cell\row 
\pard\li568\lang1049 represent one byte; a box like this :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1032\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1212\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1392\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1572\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1752\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1932\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2112\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2292 
\pard\intbl\cell\cell\cell\cell\cell\cell\cell\cell\row 
\pard\li568 represent a variable number of bytes.\par
Bytes stored within a computer do not have a "bit order", since they are always treated as a unit. However, a byte considered as an integer between 0 and 255 does have a most- and least- significant bit, and since we write numbers with the most- significant digit on the left, we also write bytes with the most-significant bit on the left. In the diagrams below, we number the bits of a byte so that bit 0 is the least-significant bit, i.e., the bits are numbered :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1932 
\pard\intbl\f2 76543210\f0\cell\row 
\pard\li568 Within a computer, a number may occupt multiple bytes. All multi-byte numbers in the format described here are stored with the MOST-significant byte fitst (at the lower memory address). For example, the decimal number 520 is stored as :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1887\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2897 
\pard\intbl\qc\f2 0\cell 1\cell\row\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1887\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2897 
\pard\intbl\qc\lang1033 00000010\par

\pard\intbl ^\par
|\par
|\par
+ more significant byte  = 2 * 256\cell 
\pard\intbl\qc 00001000\par

\pard\intbl ^\par
|\par
+ less significant byte = 8\cell\row 
\pard\li284\f0\lang1049\par
2.2. Data format\par

\pard\li568 A zlib stream has the following structure :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1377\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2882 
\pard\intbl\qc\f2 0\cell 1\cell\row\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1377\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2882 
\pard\intbl\lang1033 CMF\cell FLG (more-->)\cell\row 
\pard\li568\f0\lang1049 (if FLG.FDICT set)\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2697 
\pard\intbl\qc\f2 0 1 2 3\cell\row\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2697 
\pard\intbl\lang1033 DICTID (more-->)\cell\row 
\pard\li568\f0\lang1049\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx3237\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx4262 
\pard\intbl\f2 ...compressed data...\f0\cell ADLER32\cell\row 
\pard\li568 Any data which may appear after ADLER32 are not part of the zlib stream.\par

\pard\fi-284\li852 CMF (Compression Method and flags)\par

\pard\li852 This byte is divided inro a 4-bit compression method and a 4-bit information field depending on the compression method.\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2486\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx3376\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx5466 
\pard\intbl\f2 bits 0 to 3\f0\cell CM\cell Compression method\cell\row\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2486\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx3376\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx5466 
\pard\intbl\f2\lang1033 bits 4 to 7\cell\f0 CINFO\cell Compression info\cell\row 
\pard\li568\lang1049 CM (Compression method)\par

\pard\li852 This identifies the compression method used in the file. CM = 8 denotes the "deflate" compression method with a window size up to 32K. This is the method used by gzip and PNG (see references [\ul 1\ulnone ] and [\ul 2\ulnone ] in Chapter 3, below, for the reference documents). CM = 15 is reserved. It might be used in a future version of this specification to indicate the presence of an extra field before the compressed data.\par

\pard\li568 CINFO (Compression info)\par

\pard\li852 For CM = 8, CINFO is the base-2 logarithm of the LZ77 window size, minus eight (CINFO=7 indicates a 32K window size). Values of CINFO above 7 are not allowed in this version of the specification. CINFO is not defined in this specification for CM not equal to 8.\par

\pard\li568 FLG (FLaGs)\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 This flag byte is divided as follows :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2486\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx3346\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx6411 
\pard\intbl\f2 bits 0 to 4\cell FCHECK\cell (check bits for CMF and FLG)\cell\row\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2486\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx3346\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx6411 
\pard\intbl\lang1033 bit 5\cell FDICT\cell (preset dictionary)\cell\row\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2486\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx3346\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx6411 
\pard\intbl bits 6 to 7\cell FLEVEL\cell (compression level)\cell\row 
\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1\f0\lang1049 The FCHECK value must be such that CMF and FLG, when viewed as a 16-bit unsigned integer stored in MSB order (CMF*256 + FLG), is a multiple of 31.\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 FDICT (Preset dictionary)\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 If FDICT is set, a DICT dictionary identifier is present immediately after the FLG byte. The dictionaty is a sequence of bytes which are initially fed to the compressor without producing any compessed output. DICT is the Adler-32 checksum of this sequence of bytes (see the definition of ADLER32 below). The decompressor can use this identifier to determine which dictionary has been used by the compressor).\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 FLEVEL (Compression level)\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 These flags are available for use by specific compression methods. The "deflate" method (CM = 8) sets these flags as follows :\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1 0 - compressor used fastest algorithm\par
1 - compressor used fast algorithm\par
2 - compressor used default algorithm\par
3 - compressor used maximum compression, slowest algorithm\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 The information in FLEVEL is not needed for decompression; it is there to indicate if recompression might be worthwhile.\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 compressed data\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 Fore compression method 8, the compressed data is stored in the deflate compressed data format as described in the document "DEFLATE Compressed Data Format Specification" by L. Peter Deutsch. (See reference [\ul 3\ulnone ] in Chapter 3, below).\par
Other compressed data formats are not specified in this version of the zlib specification.\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 ADLER32 (Adler-32 checksum)\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 This contains a checksum value of the uncompressed data (excluding any dictionaty data) computed according to Adler-32 algorithm. This algotithm is a 32-bit extension and improvement of the Fletcher algorithm, used in the ITU-T X.224 / ISO 8073 standard. See references [\ul 4\ulnone ] and [\ul 5\ulnone ] in Chapter 3, below.\par
Adler-32 is composed of two sums accumulated per byte : s1 is the sum of all bytes, s2 is the sum of all s1 values. Both sums are done modulo 65521. s1 is initialized to 1, s2 to zero. The Adler-32 checksum is stored as s2*65536 + s1 in most-significant +-byte first (network) order.\par

\pard\li284\sl240\slmult1 2.3. Compliance\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 A compliant compressor must produce streams with correct CMF, FLG and ADLER32, but need not support preset dictionaries. When the zlib data format is used as part of another standard data format, the compressor may use only preset dictionaries that are specified by this other data format. If this other format does not use the preset dictionary feature, the compressor must not set the FDICT flag.\par
A compliant decompressor must check CMF, FLG and ADLER32, and provide an error indication if any of these have incorrect values. A compliant decompressor must give an error indication if CM is not one of the values defined in this specification (only the value 8 is permitted in this version), since another value could indicate the presence of new features that would cause subsequent data to be interpreted incorrectly. A compliant decompressor must give an error indication if FDICT is set and DICTID is not the identifier of a known preset dictionary. A decompressor may ignore FLEVEL and still be compliant. When the zlib data format is being used as a part of another standard format, a compliant decompressor must support all the preset dictionaries specified by the other format. When the other format does not use the preset dictionary feature, a compliant decompressor must reject any stream in which the FDICT flag is set.\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\par

